  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$",
  "worked_solution": "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$",
